  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\n594dam H5 heights spread across much of the central CONUS and Gulf \nRegion this morning with a developing trough over the western CONUS. \nAt the surface, high pressure currently sits over the Upper Midwest \nwhile a surface cyclone is developing across the Front Range. The \ndCVA with this western CONUS trough will increase across the Rockies \ntoday, allowing the surface cyclone to deepen. Surface pressure \nfalls will expand eastward into the lower Missouri River Valley, and \nwill increase low-level southerly flow through the afternoon. There \nis a boundary currently stalled across central Missouri during the \novernight hours that will start to move northward as this cyclone \ndeepens. This will continue to provide WAA and moisture transport \nthis afternoon, pushing high temperatures into the mid 90s south of \nHwy. 36. Northern Missouri and locations closer to the Iowa border \nmay remain cooler depending how far north the boundary tracks \nnorthward. A few sprinkles may occur in northern Missouri near the \nboundary but mid and upper-level lift support will be displaced \nfurther northward into Iowa, and that is where the better \nprecipitation chances will be placed for today, therefore keeping \nmost of the forecast area dry for Friday. \n\nSaturday, while the main trough starts to close up, most \ndeterministic guidance depicts a stronger H5 short-wave and \nvorticity maxima ejecting across the Plains, increasing the eastward \npropagation speed of the surface cyclone through the afternoon. \nSurface pressure falls will continue across the forecast area as \nthis occurs. Mid-level lift may increase a bit as we start to see H5 \nheight falls with the approach of this feature, presenting isolated \nto scattered shower/storm potential Saturday morning and afternoon. \nTrailing this system will be a stronger cold front that will enhance \nconvergence, especially heading into the evening hours. With \ntemperatures in the lower 90s and dewpoints in the lower 70s at \nleast, rain shower and storm activity should increase coverage \nthrough the evening, as prior daytime heating destabilizes the \nboundary layer. Mid-level cooling should help to steepen mid-level \nlapse rates, allowing the development of stronger updrafts, and a \nfew of these storms could become severe. Portions of central and \neastern Kansas may see better deep layer shear, with 0-6km bulk \nvalues around 30 kts, but unsure how far east this gets into our \nforecast area, as the mid-level trough may start to lift before \nspreading over our area. This may be a limiting factor in severe \npotential in storms on Saturday and early Sunday. Appreciable CAPE \nthrough the hail-growth zone may foster a few severe storms, and if \nwe realize PWATs approaching near 2\", may once again have a \nfavorable precipitation loading environment producing downburst wind \ngusts. As for QPF, storm totals through the weekend are generally \naround 0.5 inches, with most models remaining progressive enough \nwith the strongest storms through Sunday morning. We will need watch \nclosely overnight Saturday into Sunday morning to determine of cold \nfront may stall. Areas north of Interstate 70 earlier this week saw \nheavy amounts of rain, and may run into issues. However, if hourly \nrainfall rates remain light and occur over a longer duration into \nearly Sunday morning, hydro issues can be mitigated. The front \nshould then push completely through on Sunday, which will keep high \ntemperatures in the upper 70s to lower 80s. Perhaps far southern and \nsoutheastern zones hit the mid 80s. This will also depend on \nlingering cloud and light rainfall activity, as there may still be \nsome lift on the backside of the cold front. \n\nMonday will start a cooler period, after spending much of September \nthus far above average. The cold front will be to the southeast of \nthe area. Another H5 trough is progged to move across the Central \nCONUS this time, which may continue shower activity, with ensemble \nguidance suggesting slight chances generally between 25-35% across \nthe area. Conditions then appear drier for Tuesday and Wednesday of \nnext week, with high temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s. \nIsolated to scattered rain showers re-enter the forecast Thursday \nand Friday next week, and temperatures may start to climb back \ntoward the mid 70s.",
